The code confirms the bug (`i < stops.length - 1` skips the last stop) and the existing tests are shallow — they check for `'->'` and `'Union'` but never assert the final stop `'Eglinton'` appears, so they wouldn't catch this regression. The ACs are already clear and unambiguous, so openspec elaboration isn't warranted. However, speckit adds real value: the current test suite fails to guard against this exact bug, and hardening testability criteria (assert all stops present, single-stop edge case, empty-line no-error) is needed. My plan's preliminary belief was correct.

